WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.250
Jumanji: The Next Level is almost here, and
we've got a fresh batch of character posters

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:09.920
to get you psyched - along with one for a
mysterious new character… and another for

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:10.620
a horse.

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:11.780
"You guys!

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:12.660
We're back!

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:13.760
In Jumanji!"

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:18.630
The gang - or, rather, their avatars - are
all here: Kevin Hart's Franklin "Mouse" Finbar,

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:24.060
Karen Gillan's Ruby Roundhouse, Jack Black's
Professor Sheldon "Shelly" Oberon, Nick Jonas'

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.649
Jefferson "Seaplane" McDonough, and the absolutely
brilliantly named Dr. Smolder Bravestone,

00:00:28.649 --> 00:00:30.760
played, of course, by Dwayne Johnson.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:35.080
As for the new addition, we don't yet know
her name, or whether she's a player avatar

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:39.110
or an NPC - but she'll be portrayed by the
suddenly ubiquitous Awkwafina.

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:41.320
Oh, and did we mention a horse, you ask?

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:46.079
Yes, yes we did - and if you've seen the final
trailer for the flick, then you know that

00:00:46.079 --> 00:00:49.969
there's a darn good reason for the steed to
have its own character poster.

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:51.640
"Bethany thought you might need some help."

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:52.640
"Where is Bethany?"

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:53.880
"She's right here."

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:55.840
"She says, 'Oh my God.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:56.840
Like, I'm a total horse.'"

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:57.960
"Yup, that's Bethany."

00:00:58.720 --> 00:00:59.480
"Heeeeeey."

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.880
Just in case you're uninitiated, or need a
brief refresher: in 2017's Jumanji: Welcome

00:01:04.899 --> 00:01:09.470
to the Jungle, a group of five teens find
themselves sucked into a jungle-themed video

00:01:09.470 --> 00:01:12.270
game that must be completed before you can
escape it.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.229
Alex Wolff's Spencer finds himself controlling
Bravestone, Madison Iseman's Bethany is stuck

00:01:17.229 --> 00:01:21.980
with Professor Oberon, Ser'Darius Blain's
Anthony "Fridge" Johnson gets "Mouse" Finbar,

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:27.009
and Morgan Turner's Martha lucks out with
Ruby Roundhouse, the game's most kickass character.

00:01:27.009 --> 00:01:31.970
Together, they discover that a boy named Alex
- portrayed by Mason Guccione as a teen and

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.530
Colin Hanks as an adult - has been trapped
in the game for over 20 years, piloting the

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:38.400
avatar of "Seaplane" McDonough.

00:01:38.549 --> 00:01:42.960
They must help him to retrieve an in-game
artifact known as the "Jaguar's Eye," defeat

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:47.689
villainous NPC Russell Van Pelt - played by
Bobby Cannavale - and finish the game before

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:49.040
the jungle finishes them.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.140
This time around, the entire young cast is
returning - but there's an interesting new

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:53.640
wrinkle.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:57.920
When Spencer repairs the game, which the teens
smashed at the end of the first film, and

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:02.860
inadvertently re-enters it, his friends come
to the rescue - but unfortunately, only Martha

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.240
and Fridge make it, and Martha is the only
player controlling the same avatar as last

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:07.740
time.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.120
Fridge winds up with the portly, ineffective
Professor Oberon, and Spencer's grandfather

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:17.050
Eddie - played by Danny DeVito - and his friend
Milo, portrayed by Danny Glover, are accidentally

00:02:17.050 --> 00:02:21.240
brought along for the ride, controlling Bravestone
and Finbar, respectively.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.480
"You're Spencer's grandfather."

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:24.940
"Are we in Florida."

00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.020
"And you…"

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:27.180
"Milo Walker.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.460
Did I die… and turn into some kind of small,
muscular boy scout?"

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:36.470
The trailers reveal that this gives Hart and
Johnson room to really flex their comedic

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:41.120
chops in channeling Glover and DeVito's personas
- and that there'll be an intriguing change

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.950
of setting or two at some point, from the
jungle to the desert.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.450
The posters also indicate that our heroes
may have to contend with some bitter cold

00:02:48.450 --> 00:02:49.800
locale along the way.

00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:54.660
Case in point: this poster featuring Professor
Oberon, who looks absolutely psyched to be

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:59.020
someplace that could in no way be described
as sunny, sandy, or hot.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.840
A little bit chilly, sure, but psyched nonetheless.

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:05.220
"Weaknesses: Heat, sun, and sand.

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:08.780
How is THIS GUY a character in an adventure
game?"

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.520
Here's "Mouse," looking about as confounded
as you would be if you were a septuagenarian

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.940
grandpa who suddenly found yourself transported
into the world of one of those newfangled

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:18.840
video games.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:23.420
We only briefly hear Hart's impersonation
of Glover in the trailers, but boy, is it

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:24.520
ever spot-on.

00:03:24.740 --> 00:03:28.460
Next up: Gillan as Ruby Roundhouse, looking
as badass as ever.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.350
We can't help but point out that it will never
stop blowing our minds that this is the same

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:36.850
actress who portrays Nebula in the Marvel
Cinematic Universe, and it "only" takes about

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.750
3-5 hours in the makeup chair to render her
completely unrecognizable - and somehow even

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:42.660
more badass.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:44.680
Here's Jonas as "Seaplane" McDonough.

00:03:44.810 --> 00:03:48.860
This time around, it'll be Spencer controlling
the avatar, although Hanks will return as

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:53.580
the adult version of Alex, who had the dubious
honor of being stuck as "Seaplane" for a couple

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:54.300
of decades.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.840
Ah yes, and here's the horse… although it
is not just a horse.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.440
It's actually an avatar, and - spoiler alert
- for at least part of the movie, it's controlled

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:03.260
by Bethany.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.600
Then there's Awkwafina's new character, who
is seen only briefly in the trailer, making

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.780
it tough to parse out her motivations.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:14.400
She appears to be an NPC, and it's even possible
that she's the game's new villain, particularly

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.549
since it seems that Cannavale's Van Pelt won't
be returning for this flick.

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:22.440
She certainly looks a bit sinister, although
that could simply be misdirection.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.900
Finally, there's the smoldering Dr. Bravestone.

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.420
Remember, it's an elderly grandpa played by
Danny DeVito at the avatar's controls this

00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:33.490
time - and the trailers suggest that if you
ever had any doubts as to how gut-bustingly

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.580
funny the Rock can be, you'll soon be putting
them to rest.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:38.340
"What the hell is this?"

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:39.860
"It's your strengths and weaknesses."

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:40.870
"Smoldering intensity?"

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:42.730
"It's that stupid face you make."

00:04:42.730 --> 00:04:43.220
"Huh?"

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:49.659
Jumanji: The Next Level is directed by Jake
Kasdan, who also helmed Welcome to the Jungle;

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:54.500
he co-wrote the screenplay with returning
scribes Jeff Pinkner and Scott Rosenberg.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.400
The flick hits the big screen on December
13.

